Date: Wed, 15 Dec 1993 20:24:22 -0800
From: amara@netcom.com (Amara Graps)
Subject: Extropian Guide to Guns

Hello Extro-Beings.

I've been discussing some of the gun stuff with my SO, Vince, and he
has been very interested in what some of you have had to say. (Especially
you Canadians.. sounds grim).  Anyway, he knows many of you in the Bay
area, and wanted to contribute something, so he wrote the following
essay, which I'm forwarding for him.  Feel free to contact him at the 
email address at the end of his essay (since he's not on the list).

Amara Graps
graps@gal.arc.nasa.gov
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Dear Extropian Friends:

Just thought I'd pipe up and summarize some of the issues which have
been discussed lately.  This is more for the initiate who might be
trying to follow all the jargon, etc.., and who might be trying to
make sense out of all this for the first time.  Already noted
"experts" may wish to forego my comments.

Gun Types
---------

There are basically two kinds of guns:

  1. Pistols:  The smaller guns you can hold in your hand (i.e.
               "handguns").
  2. Rifles:   The bigger guns you usually need two hands to use.

Why the stupid definition?  Because the definitions are pretty vague,
but they do affect the laws.  There are two primary characteristics
which legally differentiate these weapon types:

  1.  Rifles tend to have longer barrels.
  2.  Pistols tend to have pistol grips (i.e. a vertical descending
      grip rather than a more horizontal one like hunting rifles.)

There are other criteria, but even these two are not used consistently
by lawmakers.  The confusion around nomenclature continues when one
attempts to classify guns according to their operation:

  1.  Single shot:    You can only shoot once before you have to
                      reload.  Many rifles and target pistols work
                      this way.
  2.  Multiple shot:  You can shoot more than once before reloading,
                      but you have to manually recock the weapon
                      between shots.  Most guns work this way,
                      including revolvers, bolt-action rifles, lever
                      action rifles, and shotguns.
  3.  Semi automatic: Not only can you shoot multiple times before
                      reloading, but the gun will recock itself
                      between shots.  You still only get one shot per
                      trigger pull, but you don't have to manually
                      recock.  Many guns work this way, including the
                      "automatic" pistols everyone has seen, and many
                      currently controversial "assult" rifles.
  4.  Full automatic: The weapon will fire multiple times with each 
                      pull of the trigger.  What might commonly be 
                      called a "machine gun".  Often, weapons of this
                      kind will offer a continuous mode (continue
                      firing while the trigger is down until ammo is
                      gone) or a selective mode (fire 2, 3, or some
                      "burst" of shots with each trigger pull.)

Much of the confusion which exists today concerns a lack of
understanding of the differences between modes (2), (3), and (4).
Most of the current controversy concerns weapons in category (3), the
semi-automatics. 

Gun Laws
--------

The following are the current major laws which affect gun purchase and
ownership.  For more info, I suggest you pick up a copy of the 1993
California gun laws, which is about $2 at most sporting or gun shops:

1.  You may not purchase or bring into CA a full-auto weapon (type 3
    above).  You may currently purchase or import any other weapon
    type. 
2.  You must wait 15 days between gun purchase and pick-up.  You must
    register the gun (Fed and state) at point of purchase.
3.  You may have a weapon in your home at any time.  You may use this
    weapon to defend yourself against life-threatening situations only
    (i.e. shooting someone for stealing your VCR is out.  You must 
    believe that they intend harm to yourself or others).
4.  Unless you have a special permit, you may not have a weapon
    outside your home (in your car, on your person, in your backpack,
    etc...) except for the following situations: 
    a) You are at a gun shop or shooting range.
    b) The gun is unloaded, in a locked case, and the ammo is in a
       separate container. (There is currently controversy over the
       definition of "separate container").
5.  To purchse a weapon (possibly just pistol), you must show proof of
    completion of a basic weapon safety course. This is currently not
    being enforced.

The following is the only new law I know of.  It will probably go into
effect within the next few months:

1.  Brady bill:  There is a Federal waiting period of 5 days between
                 gun purchase and pick-up. (CA stays at 15 days).

The following are a list of potential laws which are currently in
Congress, or are just being talked about.  There are currently no
plans to enact any of these:

1.  Feinstein rider:       Essentially all semi-auto rifles banned.  All
                           rifle and pistol clips in excess of 10 rounds
                           banned.
2.  Moynehan(?) mega-tax:  Most ammo will be taxed at some extremely high
                           rate, such as 1,000%-10,000%.  
3.  ???:                   Ban on all hollow point ammo.

To my knowledge, none of these has yet been made law, nor may they ever
become law.  Although it is prudent to be vigilant to ensure one's
freedom, the current panic over non-laws is probably extreme.

Guns for Defense
----------------

Most people want a gun for personal or home defense.  Unless you have
the proper paperwork, personal defense is kind of iffy.  Most of the
people I've asked (including cops) have told me that if you want to
carry a gun, don't get caught.  I think this was meant to be friendly,
since they were smiling at the time.  In any case, you should remember
that it is currently illegal to carry a weapon, and you should act
accordingly. 

For home defense, you can go basic or deluxe.  Basic is your "burgler
in the night" kind of thing, while deluxe is your "the riot's heading
this way" setup.  For the former, the best all-round weapon
(especially for beginners) is probably a .357 magnum revolver (like
the Smith & Wesson 586).  This is probably the best because:

1.  It will work.  Revolvers are probably a life-threatening/life-saving
    order of magnitude more reliable than autos, especially if you
    don't take care of them or really know how to use them.
2.  The .357 magnum caliber is undisputably sufficient for the job.
    It usually ranks 1st in stopping power in every test, and suffers
    none of the controversies surrounding the auto calibers.
3.  Good, reliable weapons are easily available at a range of prices.
    Good .357's range from $250 for a Taurus or Ruger to $800 for a Colt.
4.  Revolvers do not suffer from the current controversies surrounding
    semi auto weapons.  They are likely to be the longest lasting holdout
    against prohibition.

The deluxe package might include a semi auto rifle such as the Colt
Sporter in .223 or MAK-90 in 7.62x39 (or the equivalent Ruger mini-14
and mini-30).  However, these are precisely the weapons which are
currently under the threat of prohibition, so you might want to stay
away from them.  

The only "safe" rifles right now are probably bolt-action hunting
rifles. These can usually fire 4-6 shots before reloading, and can be
obtained with removable 3-5 round clips.  They cost from $350 up.  For
home defense (< 100 yard targets) they should be bought without a
scope (open sights only), in a caliber such as .308, .30-06, or .300
Winchester magnum. 

Semiauto Pistols
----------------

Unlike many pundits, I do not advocate the purchase or use of a semi
auto pistol --unless you are willing to put in the time and effort to
maintain it and learn how to use it.  If you are not, you should
probably stick with a revolver.  I say this not only in the context of
efficiency, but also one of safety.  I have had problems with all of
the semi auto pistols I have used, although it seems that these were
not really "problems" since they were common to all makes and models.

All semi autos seem to require a breaking-in period, during which time
they are prone to jam during reloading.  This jamming consists of
failure to eject a fired cartridge and reload a new one.  Jamming seems
to correlate well with the following: 

  (a) A very stiff mainspring (as is the case with a new weapon).
      This is espesially true for weapons with a shorter spring, such 
      as the smaller Sig P225 or Glock 19.  I recommend that all new
      weapons be locked open for about a week, or that you put several
      hundred rounds through them.
  (b) Insufficient ammo "kick" to operate spring.  This is also
      especially true with some of the more sophisticated ammo such as
      Black Talons, which use less powder or a lighter bullet.  This
      should not be true with +P+ loads.
  (c) Improper grip: not firm enough.  User error is by far the
      greatest commen problem.  You must hold autos much more firmly
      than revolvers. 
  (d) Insufficient lubrication or physical jamming.  I've cured
      jamming on a new gun just by adding some oil.  The normal, oxidized
      and annealed state of oiled surfaces may not be enough for some auto
      weapons, especially when caked with soot.  Be prepared to check, wipe,
      and oil your pistols regularly. 

Other problems occur with clips or choice of ammo.  In any case, it's
essential to test all your equipment before you depend on it.  I don't
know how much other people go shooting, but given my experiences I'm
worried that there may be a substantial fraction out there who will be
surprised to find that they only get one shot off when they need it.

Glocks
------

All the Glock pistols are excellent weapons, and I would recommend
them to anyone.  I like the Glock 17 better than the Sig P226, if that
means anything.  The Glocks are sturdy, easy to use and clean,
accurate, and cheap(er).  They do have a very different trigger pull than
other autos or revolvers, and this pull may feel too "squishy" to some
people.  They are also made with plastic bodies to a fairly Spartan
design, so may not appeal to some people aesthetically.

Depending on semantics, the Glocks are either both single and double
action, or neither.  What is true is the following: the Glocks have no
manual safety (as does a standard single-action pistol).  Once cocked,
a trigger pull will fire the weapon (like the double-actions).
However, this pull is much lighter than the "standard" double action
pull.  Therefore, I classify them as single-action pistols in terms of
safety.

Ammo
----

During the current hoopla concerning high-class ammo, one should
remember that you can kill someone with a .22, a knife, a car, or your
bare hands.  If you successfully shoot someone with any of today's
modern weapons, you will do them damage.  How many shots are required
is problematic, and may be more an indication of your marksman skills
than your ammo.

Nevertheless, to the extent that it does matter, the following are
clear: 

  1.  Revolvers in calibers >= .357 magnum are the most effective
      weapons.  This includes .44 magnum and .45, but probably not .38 or
      .38 Special.  Standard loads for these calibers include semi-jacketed
      hollowpoint loads.
  2.  Auto pistol calibers are "less" effective, and rank roughly with
      size: 9mm-->.45.  In almost all cases and tests, auto calibers are
      much more effective when used with hollowpoint expanding bullets.
      This improvement is especially true for 9mm, which is only marginally
      effective in non-hollow point loads.

Current prices for round-nose 9mm ammo is $9-12 for a box of 50.
Current prices for Black Talon ammo is $15 (regular)-$50 (current LA
price) for a box of 20, if you can find them.  Other brands/products
such as Gold Sable, Hydra Shock, and other hollow points are roughly
equivalent and are still available.  As with all equipment, make sure
you check the hollow points you use with your pistol and its clips
before you bet your life on them.

Children
--------

Children and guns do not mix.  No amount of rational dialog will keep
a 3 year old (let alone a 16 year old) out of your weapons.  All
weapons, loaded or unloaded, should be kept locked when childeren are
around.  This is especially true for a pistol such as the Glock, which
is, in my opinion, substantially less safe for children than others.

It is currently federal law that you are responsible for the actions
and fate of a child who has access to one of your weapons.

