We need to carefully distinguish between "prohibition of sale," which
is being talked about, and "prohibition of ownership," i.e.,
confiscation. Although a few extremist gun-banners are talking
confiscation, the proposed legislation is about banning the further
sale of some classes of guns. Granted, this might someday become
confiscation.

But a person desiring a semi-auto of the sort that makes Hilary and
Sarah scream in terror should not shy away from buying one now while
they are still legal.

And don't forget to consider shotguns. Vince did not include them in
his taxonomy of guns, but they are fine as home defense weapons. A bit
hard to wield in tight quarters, like hallways, but generally
excellent. A 12-gauge pump, like the Remington 870 with an 18" barrel is
the way to go, I think.

Vince urges folks to pick revolvers over semiautomatics. Well, the gun
mags debate this every couple of issues, so I won't here. 

>   2.  Auto pistol calibers are "less" effective, and rank roughly with

I know Vince says "less," suggesting ambiguity, but I still disagree.
For the same round, e.g. a .45 ACP, the semiauto will give slightly
_higher_ velocities than a revolver will (unless the revolver has a
noticeably longer barrel, e.g., 6"). The reason is the tighter gas
seal and the lack of a cylinder gap.

The 9mm is a short cartridge, and a fairly light bullet (110-125 grains is
common), compared to the longer cartridge of the .357 magnum and the
heavier bullet (typically 125-160 grains). More powder, etc.

But a 10mm semiauto, for example, is the equal of a hot .357 magnum, I
am quite sure.

(I'd _still_ recommend a Glock, SIG, Firestar, H&K, Smith and Wesson
semiauto in 9mm to those of you out there. Tests show a good
load--Cor-Bon, Hydra-Shok, Silvertip, etc.--will have a high
percentage of "one-shot stops." Maybe not as high as a 10mm or a .44
magnum, but still more than 70-80%.)

