Subject: Kids these days

Dean Tribble writes:
>		 To me, being "fully human" means having the entire range of
>	 human experience, including death.  No, thanks.
>
>I might try that sometime, as long as it's not fatal.

People have been giving me dire warnings about what my life will be like when
my kids become teenagers.  Frankly, that prospect doesn't worry me
particularly, as I've always been able to communicate with teenagers.  However,
it got me to thinking about how it seems like every generation of kids finds
things to do that shock and upset their parents (this being a large part of the
point of much teenage activity).  Many of these things are stupid and self
destructive but not ultimately fatal for the majority of the generation.

And so, the question:

What will happen when some of us nanotechnologically spiffed up transhuman
parents wind up with nanotechnologically spiffed up transhuman offspring?  What
will *they* come up with to shock, amaze and dismay their elders?  And don't
tell me it won't happen because some wonderful transcendent property of the
hyper-rational transhuman intellect make them be less stupid.  I doubt better
technology is going to make rebellious behavior less fun.  Maybe more fun, but
certainly not less.

So how about it?

The example I thought of was skydiving without a parachute.  Of course we could
fix ourselves up so that we could fly around or plunge into the ground without
injury, but that's the sort of thing us old fogeys would do.  No, it would be
way more radical to jump out of the plane, splat into the ground, and die.  One
could posit running incremental recording of brain state so that the experience
is not lost when they reconstruct the kid from backups, but once again that's
thinking like one of us eager-to-learn experience junkies.  But kids don't do
stuff like that for the experience; they do it be cool, to impress their
friends and upset the grownups.  They're not going to be bothered by the loss
of a few hours of brain state if it earns them a rep.  And this example is
probably tame compared to whatever the reality will actually end up being.

I'm sure some of you could think of better examples.


